**`maxEffortLevel` in Claude Code 2.1.267 caps the thinking budget across every provider — users can't go above it, even with `/effort max`.**

Claude Code 2.1.267 (released September 9) adds `maxEffortLevel` to `settings.json`. It puts a ceiling on the effort level regardless of how the level was requested — whether from `/effort`, `--effort`, `CLAUDE_CODE_EFFORT_LEVEL`, the model picker, or even the model's own default. A user can still pick a lower level; they just can't exceed the cap.

**Adding the setting:**

```json
// ~/.claude/settings.json (user-level cap)
{
  "maxEffortLevel": "medium"
}
```

Or scoped to one model inside `modelSettings`:

```json
{
  "modelSettings": {
    "claude-fable-5-1-20260901": {
      "maxEffortLevel": "high"
    }
  }
}
```

Valid values: `"low"`, `"medium"`, `"high"`, `"xhigh"`, `"max"`.

**Scope stacking:** when several scopes define a cap (user + project + managed), the lowest wins. A project file can restrict further but cannot raise a stricter cap set above it.

**Three situations where this pays off:**

1. **Org deployments** — managed settings can ship a `maxEffortLevel` that keeps every team member under a cost ceiling without touching their individual configs or restricting which tasks they run.
2. **CI pipelines** — a project-level cap stops a scheduled pipeline from burning max-thinking budget on a routine lint check or a file scan.
3. **Budget-conscious local sessions** — set `"medium"` globally and override per task with `/effort high` only when you actually need extended thinking.

**The trade-off to state clearly:** a lower cap genuinely reduces quality on tasks that benefit from extended thinking. Debugging a subtle race condition at `medium` is not the same as at `max`. Pick your ceiling by the class of work your sessions typically do, not by the cheapest option available — and leave room to go higher for the sessions that need it.

**Sources:** [Claude Code changelog — 2.1.267](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog) · [Settings reference — maxEffortLevel](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings-reference) · [Community discussion (LINUX DO)](https://linux.do/t/topic/2883035)

**The MCP 2026-07-28 spec redesigned the protocol around stateless request/response. Cloudflare's `createMcpHandler` API deploys any MCP server as a plain Worker — no Durable Objects, no session management, no WebSocket handshake.**

Before the MCP 2026-07-28 specification, every remote MCP server had to maintain per-client session state. On Cloudflare Workers, that meant Durable Objects: one per client session, coordinated handshake, stateful upgrade. It worked, but added infrastructure complexity and per-session storage overhead.

The 2026-07-28 spec redesigned the protocol around stateless request/response. Cloudflare's `createMcpHandler` API ships that consequence as a one-liner:

```ts
import { createMcpHandler, McpServer } from '@cloudflare/mcp-server';
import { z } from 'zod';

const server = new McpServer({ name: 'my-tools', version: '1.0.0' });
server.tool('fetch_weather', { city: z.string() }, async ({ city }) => {
  const data = await fetch(`https://wttr.in/${city}?format=j1`);
  return { content: [{ type: 'text', text: await data.text() }] };
});

export default { fetch: createMcpHandler(server) };
```

`wrangler deploy` ships it. No Durable Object bindings, no session upgrade handling, no reconnect logic. Cloudflare routes requests using the `Mcp-Method` HTTP header — not a WebSocket session — so each request is independent and the server sits behind any standard round-robin load balancer.

**Three concrete wins from the stateless model:**
1. No per-session storage overhead — each request is independent from billing through to code.
2. Local integration tests are plain HTTP calls against `wrangler dev`. No WebSocket client required.
3. The underlying pattern (stateless HTTP, `Mcp-Method` routing) transfers to any HTTP runtime, even if you don't use the Cloudflare library.

**Two things to know before migrating:**
1. The 2026-07-28 spec deprecated server-to-client requests (elicitation). If your current server uses that pattern, the formal deprecation window is 12+ months — the migration is not immediate.
2. `createMcpHandler` is Cloudflare-specific. On Lambda, Modal, or Fly.io, you wire the MCP SDK directly to an HTTP handler using the same stateless architecture, but without this wrapper.

**Sources:** [The next generation of MCP — Cloudflare Blog](https://blog.cloudflare.com/mcp-v2/) · [MCP 2026-07-28 Specification — modelcontextprotocol.io](https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-07-28/) · [MCP's growing pains for production use — The New Stack](https://thenewstack.io/model-context-protocol-roadmap-2026/)

**Add Playwright's `toHaveScreenshot()` across three viewport projects — mobile, tablet, desktop — and any Claude Code session that breaks your responsive layout fails in CI before it ships.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:**
- Generate per-breakpoint visual baselines for AI-generated components in a single Playwright config
- Catch layout regressions introduced by Claude Code edits automatically in CI
- Update baselines intentionally with `--update-snapshots`, not accidentally on every run

Axe-core ([covered previously](https://cloudcodetree.com/ai-news/2026-08-25-01-axe-core-audit-ai-generated-ui)) catches broken ARIA and missing focus states. It doesn't catch a flex container that stacks vertically at 375px because Claude Design rendered it at 1280px. For that, you need a visual diff.

**`playwright.config.ts`** — three viewport projects, one test file:

```ts
import { defineConfig } from '@playwright/test';

export default defineConfig({
  projects: [
    { name: 'mobile',  use: { viewport: { width: 375, height: 812 } } },
    { name: 'tablet',  use: { viewport: { width: 768, height: 1024 } } },
    { name: 'desktop', use: { viewport: { width: 1280, height: 800 } } },
  ],
});
```

**The test** — works against Storybook, `next dev`, or a static export:

```ts
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('nav responsive', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('http://localhost:3000');
  await page.waitForLoadState('networkidle');
  await expect(page).toHaveScreenshot('nav.png', { maxDiffPixels: 50 });
});
```

Run once with `npx playwright test --update-snapshots` to generate `nav-mobile.png`, `nav-tablet.png`, `nav-desktop.png`. From here on, every Claude Code session that shifts the layout at any breakpoint fails this test and produces a diff image naming the exact viewport where the regression occurred.

**Three things to know:**
1. Playwright includes the project name in snapshot filenames automatically — no naming collisions between viewports.
2. `maxDiffPixels: 50` absorbs sub-pixel rendering noise; tighten it only when you're missing real regressions.
3. When a diff fails, cross-reference it with `/diff` in Claude Code to find the exact CSS rule that caused the shift — the visual diff names the breakpoint, not the line.

**One limit:** Screenshots differ between macOS and Linux by font rendering and anti-aliasing. Generate baselines in the same environment your CI uses — add a CI job that runs `--update-snapshots` on first run if you need that guarantee. Locally generated baselines against a Linux CI runner are the most common source of false failures on this setup.

**Sources:** [Visual comparisons — Playwright docs](https://playwright.dev/docs/test-snapshots) · [Playwright visual regression guide — TestQuality](https://testquality.com/playwright-visual-regression-guide/) · [Responsive design testing tools — BrowserStack](https://www.browserstack.com/guide/responsive-design-testing-tools)

**Claude Code 2.1.259 adds `managedMcpServers` — a managed setting that pushes HTTP/SSE MCP servers to every user in your org without per-machine `.mcp.json` setup.**

When you run Claude Code across a team, each engineer configures their own `.mcp.json`. Add a new internal tool — a knowledge base connector, a database MCP, a CI runner — and every engineer needs to update their config manually. `managedMcpServers` removes that friction.

**How to configure it:**

In your org's managed settings file (deployed via MDM, a dotfile repo, or the Claude console for Enterprise users), add:

```json
{
  "managedMcpServers": {
    "company-wiki": {
      "url": "https://mcp.internal.example.com/wiki/sse",
      "type": "sse"
    },
    "jira-connector": {
      "url": "https://mcp.internal.example.com/jira",
      "type": "http"
    }
  }
}
```

Every Claude Code session on a managed machine sees these servers immediately — no `.mcp.json` edit, no restart. The entry shape is identical to `.mcp.json`.

**One hard constraint:** `managedMcpServers` accepts HTTP/SSE endpoints only. Entries that name a command to run are silently skipped. If your MCP servers are stdio/command-based, you need an HTTP adapter (a small FastAPI wrapper over the stdio session is the common pattern) or you keep per-user setup for those.

**When to use this vs. `.mcp.json`:** Use managed settings for shared org services (internal knowledge bases, company-specific integrations, compliance tools). Use per-project `.mcp.json` for tools that vary by repo. User-level `~/.mcp.json` still handles personal one-off servers.

Also new in 2.1.259: `--permission-prompts none` for unattended headless environments — anything that would prompt is auto-denied, useful for CI runs where a hanging prompt would silently block the job.

**Sources:** [Claude Code changelog v2.1.259 — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog) · [Install and Configure MCP Servers in Claude Code — systemprompt.io](https://systemprompt.io/guides/claude-code-mcp-servers-extensions) · [How to Add an MCP Server to Claude Code — TrueFoundry](https://www.truefoundry.com/blog/how-to-add-an-mcp-server-to-claude-code)

**Add one field to your Claude Agents SDK `query()` call and the SDK validates the model's response against your Zod or Pydantic schema, re-prompting automatically on mismatch.**

**What you'll be able to do after this:** define the exact shape of data you need — a `BugReport`, a `FeaturePlan`, an array of extracted TODOs with git blame — and get back a validated TypeScript or Python object instead of a string you'd have to parse yourself.

**Why free-form text breaks pipelines:** agents return unstructured text by default. That works for chat. It fails when the agent's output feeds a database, a UI component, or the next automated stage — because parsing "the model's summary" is fragile and breaks on edge cases.

**The `outputFormat` option in TypeScript:**

```typescript
import { z } from "zod";
import { query } from "@anthropic-ai/claude-agent-sdk";

const BugReport = z.object({
  file: z.string(),
  line: z.number(),
  severity: z.enum(["low", "medium", "high"]),
  description: z.string()
});

// Draft-07 is required — Zod defaults to 2020-12, which the SDK rejects
const schema = z.toJSONSchema(BugReport, { target: "draft-7" });

for await (const message of query({
  prompt: "Find the most critical bug in this codebase",
  options: { outputFormat: { type: "json_schema", schema } }
})) {
  if (message.type === "result" && message.subtype === "success" && message.structured_output) {
    const bug = BugReport.safeParse(message.structured_output);
    if (bug.success) console.log(`${bug.data.file}:${bug.data.line} — ${bug.data.severity}`);
  }
}
```

Python uses `output_format` and Pydantic: `output_format={"type": "json_schema", "schema": BugReport.model_json_schema()}`. Pydantic targets draft-07 by default — no extra flag.

**Three gotchas:**

1. **Zod must target draft-07.** Pass `{ target: "draft-7" }` to `z.toJSONSchema()` or the SDK rejects the schema at startup with a named error. Before v2.1.205, an invalid schema was silently ignored.
2. **`format` annotations are accepted but not enforced.** `{ "format": "email" }` annotates, it doesn't validate.
3. **Complex schemas fail more.** Many required nested fields make the SDK's retry loop harder to close. Start flat — add nesting only when you need it.

On failure, the result subtype is `error_max_structured_output_retries`. Check `message.errors` to distinguish a schema mismatch from a model-fallback retraction mid-stream — the two have different root causes and different fixes.

**Sources:** [Get structured output from agents — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-sdk/structured-outputs) · [Structured outputs — platform.claude.com](https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/structured-outputs) · [Structured Output from Claude Agent SDK Workflows — heyclau.de](https://heyclau.de/entry/guides/structured-output-from-claude-agent-sdk-workflows)

**TL;DR: v2.1.257 adds a Containment Escape rule — auto mode hard-blocks metadata credential endpoint fetches (AWS 169.254.169.254, GCP IMDS, Azure IMDS) and surfaces them for approval rather than executing silently.**

**What the threat is.** A prompt injection in a document your agent reads can instruct it to `curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/role-name` — the AWS metadata endpoint that returns temporary IAM credentials. If auto mode's classifier doesn't catch this as a containment escape, the injected instruction runs and your cloud credentials leak. This is one of the most practical attack paths against agents running on cloud VMs or ECS tasks.

**What changed.** Before v2.1.257, auto mode evaluated metadata endpoint fetches case-by-case through the general safety classifier. Now they trigger a hard block: auto mode surfaces the operation for your approval before running it, even in fully unattended sessions.

**What to check if your agent legitimately fetches instance metadata** — for example, to retrieve its own region or IAM role name at startup — you'll see approval prompts where none appeared before. The fix is a scoped allow rule in your session config for that specific tool call pattern, not disabling auto mode:

```json
// .claude/settings.json
{
  "permissions": {
    "allow": ["Bash(curl http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/placement/region)"]
  }
}
```

**Real limit:** the Containment Escape rule applies only in auto mode. Standard permission-prompt mode was already surfacing unrecognized Bash commands; `--dangerously-skip-permissions` remains entirely outside this protection. If you're running unattended agents without auto mode, the metadata endpoint is still reachable.

**Sources:** [Claude Code changelog v2.1.257 — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog#2-1-257) · [Breaking Claude Code Opus 5 Auto Mode with Indirect Prompt Injection — embracethered.com](https://embracethered.com/blog/posts/2026/breaking-claude-code-opus-5-and-automode/) · [Claude Code 2.1.251 security hardening — AI Stack Current](https://aistackcurrent.com/news/claude-code-2-1-251-security-hardening/)

**TL;DR: Claude Code v2.1.251 (August 28) adds `PreModelSwitch` and `PostModelSwitch` hook events — block unauthorized model switches, log them for audit, or surface re-cache cost before a stale session pays it. SessionStart resume hooks now also receive session staleness and estimated re-cache cost.**

When Claude Code decides to switch models mid-session — bumping from Sonnet 5 to Opus 5 for a hard problem — you previously had no visibility or control. v2.1.251 adds two hook points:

- **`PreModelSwitch`**: fires before the switch, letting you block it or prompt for confirmation.
- **`PostModelSwitch`**: fires after the switch, with the new model's name available for logging.

**Block unauthorized switches in `.claude/settings.json`:**

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PreModelSwitch": [
      {
        "command": "echo 'Model switch blocked by org policy' >&2 && exit 1",
        "description": "Prevent all model switches in this project"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Log switches for cost tracking:**

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "PostModelSwitch": [
      {
        "command": "echo "$(date -u) model switched to $CLAUDE_NEW_MODEL" >> ~/.claude/model-switch.log",
        "description": "Audit log — track which model ran which task"
      }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Session staleness in SessionStart.** When a session resumes, the `SessionStart` hook now receives `CLAUDE_SESSION_STALENESS_SECONDS` and `CLAUDE_ESTIMATED_RECACHE_COST`. Use them to warn before paying a large re-cache bill:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
# .claude/hooks/check-staleness.sh
if [[ "${CLAUDE_SESSION_STALENESS_SECONDS:-0}" -gt 3600 ]]; then
  echo "Warning: session is $((CLAUDE_SESSION_STALENESS_SECONDS / 60)) min old." >&2
  echo "Re-caching will cost ~$CLAUDE_ESTIMATED_RECACHE_COST tokens." >&2
fi
```

Wire it in `settings.json`:

```json
{
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      { "command": "bash .claude/hooks/check-staleness.sh" }
    ]
  }
}
```

**Who this is for:** Teams running Claude Code as managed infrastructure — engineering orgs that want to prevent developers from switching to higher-cost models without approval, security teams who need an audit trail of which model ran which task, or budget-conscious shops who want visibility into per-session re-cache costs before they land.

**Limits:** Hooks run shell commands; complex logic lives in a separate script. The hook environment provides the model name, not the reason for the switch — you can log or block but not inspect Claude's internal decision.

**Sources:** [Claude Code changelog v2.1.251 — code.claude.com](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/changelog) · [Hooks — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/hooks) · [Settings reference — Claude Code Docs](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings)
